  "figure_captions": {
    "prediction_error_stationary.pdf": "Stationary-plane velocity prediction error $\\mathcal E_t(D_t)$ in~\\eqref{eq:contact-prediction-error}, in $(\\mathrm{mm}/\\mathrm{s})^2$. Blue, orange and pink solid curves denote $\\overline P_t^+$, $P_{\\widehat{\\mathcal X}_t}$ and $\\overline P_t^{\\mathrm{iso}}$, respectively. Each curve joins arithmetic means over 256 Monte Carlo simulations at the four saved indices $t=1,4,16,32$. Blue shading gives pointwise Student 95\\% confidence intervals for the posterior-mean averages.",
    "prediction_error_sphere.pdf": "Moving-sphere velocity prediction error $\\mathcal E_t(D_t)$ in~\\eqref{eq:contact-prediction-error}, in $(\\mathrm{mm}/\\mathrm{s})^2$. Blue, orange and pink solid curves denote the posterior mean $\\overline P_t^+$, mode $P_{\\widehat{\\mathcal X}_t}$ and matched isotropic mean $\\overline P_t^{\\mathrm{iso}}$ with measured transport $G_t=R_t^\\top R_{t-1}$. The dark gray solid curve is the posterior mean with $G_t=I_3$. Each curve joins arithmetic means over the same 256 Monte Carlo simulations at all 48 original action indices $t=4,8,\\ldots,192$. Blue shading gives pointwise Student 95\\% confidence intervals for the measured-transport posterior-mean averages. The ordering varies with time; there is no programmed event at $t=128$.",
    "prediction_error_references.pdf": "Time-averaged sphere velocity prediction error from~\\eqref{eq:contact-prediction-error}, in $(\\mathrm{mm}/\\mathrm{s})^2$. Blue, orange, pink, brown, green and light blue points denote the posterior mean, mode, matched isotropic mean, regularized matrix $\\widetilde M_t$, GREAT (sliding window) and GREAT (exponential weighting), respectively. Each point averages $\\mathcal E_t(D_t)$ over all 48 action indices $t=4,8,\\ldots,192$ within each simulation and then over 256 Monte Carlo simulations. Horizontal bars are Student 95\\% confidence intervals for these means. Here $\\widetilde M_t=\\rho P_{\\widehat{\\mathcal X}_t}+(1-\\rho)(2/3)I_3$.",
    "transport_paired_rise.pdf": "Empirical distributions of the paired endpoint change $\\Delta\\mathcal E=\\mathcal E_{192}(D_{192})-\\mathcal E_{128}(D_{128})$, in $(\\mathrm{mm}/\\mathrm{s})^2$. Blue and dark gray curves use the posterior mean with measured transport $G_t=R_t^\\top R_{t-1}$ and with $G_t=I_3$, respectively. Each empirical cumulative distribution retains all 256 simulation-level changes, including both tails; positive values indicate a larger error at $t=192$ in the same simulation. These are empirical distributions, with no confidence band. The vertical dashed line marks zero change, not a motion event.",
    "transport_projector_change.pdf": "Changes of the generating tangent-plane projector during sphere motion. Blue shows $\\|P_t-P_{t-1}\\|_{\\mathrm F}$ in sensor coordinates; dark gray shows $\\|P_t-G_tP_{t-1}G_t^\\top\\|_{\\mathrm F}$ after compensating the measured frame rotation. Solid curves are arithmetic means over 256 Monte Carlo simulations at the 191 indices $t=2,3,\\ldots,192$; the initial normal and resulting physical path vary across simulations. No confidence band is displayed. The compensated increment equals the world-frame projector increment within each simulation.",
    "transport_excitation.pdf": "Directional excitation of the known inputs. Let $S_t=\\beta G_tS_{t-1}G_t^\\top+v_tv_t^\\top$, $s_t=\\beta s_{t-1}+1$, and $H_t=S_t/(\\sigma^2s_t)$, with $S_0=0$, $s_0=0$ and the original $\\beta=0.9$. Blue, orange and pink solid curves give the increasingly ordered eigenvalues $\\lambda_1(H_t)$, $\\lambda_2(H_t)$ and $\\lambda_3(H_t)$, evaluated at the 48 action indices $t=4,8,\\ldots,192$. The vertical scale is logarithmic. The input and measured-frame schedules are shared deterministic quantities, so these curves are not Monte Carlo error means and no uncertainty band is drawn.",
    "transport_curvature.pdf": "Tangent curvature of the normal posterior during sphere motion. For increasingly ordered eigenvalues of the plane parameter $A_t^+$, define $c_{\\min,t}=2[\\lambda_2(A_t^+)-\\lambda_1(A_t^+)]$ and $c_{\\max,t}=2[\\lambda_3(A_t^+)-\\lambda_1(A_t^+)]$. Blue and orange curves give these curvatures with measured transport; dark gray and light gray give the minimum and maximum with $G_t=I_3$. Curves are arithmetic means over 256 Monte Carlo simulations at the 48 action indices $t=4,8,\\ldots,192$; matching bands give pointwise Student 95\\% confidence intervals for the means. These are angular tangent curvatures of the maintained normal posterior, rather than prediction-error or coverage estimates."
  },
